{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","count":16,"questions":[{"id":51253,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following, the correct statement is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></math> decreases when solid ammonium chloride is added to a dilute aqueous solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></math> decreases when solid sodium acetate is added to a dilute aqueous solution of acetic acid","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></math> decreases when solid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></math> is added to a dilute aqueous solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>H</mi></math>"," <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></math> decreases when solid sodium oxalate is added to a dilute aqueous solution of oxalic acid"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p> Dil.aqueous solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>O</mi><mo> </mo><mi>H</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>O</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⇌</mo><mi>N</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>On adding solid ammonium chloride</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⟶</mo><mi>N</mi><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The reaction moves backward due to common ion effect. The concentration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> decreases and hence the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></math> decreases.</p>\n\n<p> </p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3f7dedfe569a27c3a56"},{"id":51254,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$\\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is precipitated when $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ is added to a solution of $\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$. If the final concentration of $\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ is $10^{-10} \\mathrm{M}$, the concentration of $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}(\\mathrm{M})$ in the solution is\n[Solubility product for $\\left.\\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}=5.6 \\times 10^{-12}\\right]$","options":["$0.056$","$0.12$","$0.24$","$0.025$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{K}_{\\text {sp }} \\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}=\\left[\\mathrm{Mg}^{+2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]^{2}5.6 \\times 10^{-12}=\\left[10^{-10}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]^{2}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\sqrt{5.6 \\times 10^{-2}}=0.24 \\mathrm{M}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213062e16162b894922"},{"id":51255,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid solution is closest to [Dissociation constant of acid $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$ ]","options":["$2.87$","$1.00$","$2.07$","$4.76$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}} \\cdot \\mathrm{c}}=\\sqrt{1.8 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 0.1}=\\sqrt{1.8} \\times 10^{-3}\\mathrm{pH}=3-\\log (1.34)=2.87$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2134faeaf6bdf32e6f5"},{"id":51256,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The most stable Lewis acid-base adduct among the following is","options":["$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{~N} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{P} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BCl}_{3}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Greater is the tendency to donate $\\ell$.p more stable will be the lewis. acid-acid-base adduct.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2134f47446b99efdf30"},{"id":51257,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The solubility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>B</mtext><mtext>a</mtext><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>4</mn></msub></math> in pure water (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>g</mtext><msup><mtext>L</mtext><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> ) is closest to<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"[\"><mrow></mrow></mfenced></math>Given; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>K</mtext><mtext>sp</mtext></msub></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>B</mtext><mtext>a</mtext><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>°</mo><mtext>C</mtext><mo>.</mo></math> Molecular weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>B</mtext><mtext>a</mtext><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>233</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>g</mtext><mo> </mo><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>o</mtext><msup><mtext>l</mtext><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> ]","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>K</mtext><mtext>sp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mtext>BaSO</mtext><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⇌</mo><msup><mtext>Ba</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mtext>SO</mtext><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mspace></mspace></mtd></mtr></mtable><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mtext>S</mtext><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mtext>S</mtext></math></p>\n\n<p>Let the solubity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>B</mtext><msup><mtext>a</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>S</mtext><msubsup><mtext>O</mtext><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>S</mtext></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mtext>K</mtext><mrow><mtext>s</mtext><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mtext>S</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mtext>S</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>S</mtext><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>o</mtext><mtext>l</mtext><mo>/</mo><mtext>L</mtext></math></p>\n\n<p>Thus, solubility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>BaSO</mtext><mn>4</mn></msub></math> in pure water (in g/L)</p>\n\n<p>Solubility (in mol/L) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>×</mo></math> Molecular weight</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>233</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3f797350fd273dcdf3a"},{"id":51258,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The pH of $1 \\mathrm{~N}$ aqueous solutions of $\\mathrm{HCl}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOh}$ and HCOOH follows the order","options":["$\\mathrm{HCl}\\gt \\mathrm{HCOOH}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$","$\\mathrm{CHl}=\\mathrm{HCOOH}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$","$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\gt \\mathrm{HCOOH}\\gt \\mathrm{HCl}$","$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}=\\mathrm{HCOOH}\\gt \\mathrm{HCl}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{HCl}\\gt \\mathrm{HCOOH}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH} ;$ Order of Acidic strength","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21330ee1d5c7eb4a95c"},{"id":51259,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Solubility products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CuI</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Ag</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> have almost the same value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mo>~</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>. The ratio of solubilities of the two salts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>CuI</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>Ag</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> is closest to ","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>03</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>10</mn></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CuI</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mfenced><mo>⇌</mo><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mfenced><mi>aq</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mfenced><mi>aq</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>If its solubility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>, at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>equilibrium</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>,</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Since, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Ag</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mfenced><mo>⇌</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mfenced><mi>aq</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>CrO</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mfenced><mi>aq</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msubsup><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfenced></math><br />\nIf its solubility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></math>, at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>eqbm</mi><mo>.</mo></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msubsup><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>Since, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>sp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></math>,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></math>,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da702248d7845b04e5bee"},{"id":51260,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A weak acid is titrated with a weak base. Consider the following statements regarding the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> of the solution at the equivalence point:<br />\n(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> depends on the concentration of acid and base.<br />\n(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> is independent of the concentration of acid and base<br />\n(iii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> depends on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> of acid and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub></math> of base.<br />\n(iv) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> is independent of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> of acid and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub></math> of base.<br />\nThe correct statements are:-","options":["only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>iii</mi><mo>)</mo></math>","only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>iv</mi><mo>)</mo></math>","only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>iii</mi><mo>)</mo></math>","only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>iv</mi><mo>)</mo></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"For salts of weak acid and weak base<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> is independent of concentration of acid and base","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7489aa31bf86cbc8a3a"},{"id":51261,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The degree of dissociation of acetic acid $\\left(0.1 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}\\right)$ in water $\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}\\right.$ of acetic acid is $\\left.10^{-5}\\right)$ is","options":["$0.01$","$0.5$","$0.1$","$1.0$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{ll}\\mathrm{C}=0.1 \\mathrm{M} & \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=10^{-5} \\\\ \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\alpha^{2} \\mathrm{C} \\\\ 10^{-5}=\\alpha^{2} \\times 0.1 & \\alpha^{2}=10^{-4} \\\\ & \\alpha=10^{-2}\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21365e0defa5402f6c0"},{"id":51262,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Upon mixing equal volumes of aqueous solutions of $0.1 \\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{HCl}$ and $0.2 \\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$, the concentration of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$in the resulting solution is $-$","options":["$0.30 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$","$0.25 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$","$0.15 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$","$0.10 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$0.1 \\mathrm{MHCl}, \\mathrm{V}$ volume\n$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$moles $=0.1 \\mathrm{~V}0.2 \\mathrm{MH}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}, \\mathrm{~V}$ volume\n$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$moles $=0.2 \\times 2 \\times \\mathrm{V}=0.4 \\mathrm{~V}$\nTotal moles of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}=0.4 \\mathrm{~V}+0.1 \\mathrm{~V}=0.5 \\mathrm{~V}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\frac{\\text { Moles }}{\\text { Vol. }}=\\frac{0.5 \\mathrm{~V}}{2 \\mathrm{~V}}=0.25 \\mathrm{M} / \\mathrm{I}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21395e96670fa8d711c"},{"id":51263,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An aqueous buffer is prepared by adding $100 \\mathrm{ml}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{t}^{-1}$ acetic acid to $50 \\mathrm{ml}$ of $0.2 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{I}^{-1}$ of sodium acetate. If $\\mathrm{pKa}$ of acetic acid is $4.76$, the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the buffer is","options":["$4.26$","$5.76$","$3.76$","$4.76$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Meq of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}=100 \\times 0.1 \\times 1=10$\nMeq of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}=50 \\times 0.2 \\times 1=10\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{pH}=\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}+\\log \\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\right]} \\\\\n\\mathrm{pH}=4.76+\\log \\frac{10}{10} \\\\\n\\mathrm{pH}=4.76+\\log 1 \\\\\n\\mathrm{pH}=4.76\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21310e318976a398783"},{"id":51264,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The ubility product of $\\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is $1.0 \\times 10^{-12}$. Concentrated aqueous $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ ution is added to a $0.01 \\mathrm{M}$ aqueous ution of $\\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$. The $\\mathrm{pH}$ at which precipitation occur is-","options":["$7.2$","$7.8$","$8.0$","$9.0$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\quad \\mathrm{MgCl}_{2} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Mg}^{+2}+2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\quad ? \\\\\n0.01 \\mathrm{M} \\\\\n\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{Q}=\\left[\\mathrm{Mg}^{+2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]^{2} \\\\\n10^{-12}=[0.01]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]^{2} \\\\\n{\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]^{2}=10^{-10}} \\\\\n{\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=10^{-5}} \\\\\n\\mathrm{pOH}=5 \\quad \\therefore \\mathrm{pH}=9\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213cbe39908abcdae0e"},{"id":51265,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A buffer solution can be prepared by mixing equal volumes of","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><mi>HCl</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><mi>HCl</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><mi>NaOH</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><mi>HCl</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>The solutions which resist change in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> on dilution or with the addition of small amounts of acid or alkali are called buffer solution. It is a mixture of weak base with its salt or weak acid with its salt.</p>\n\n<p>Thus, buffer solution can be prepared by mixing equal volumes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> (weak base) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><mi>HCl</mi></math> (strong acid). It results in the formation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> (weak base) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi></math> (salt of weak base <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> ), which is a basic buffer mixture.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68f3ee750b6b0d420d8"},{"id":51266,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"If the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> concentration of an aqueous solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>001</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>,</mo></math>, then the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pOH</mi></math> of the solution would be","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>001</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>999</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><msup><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo></msup></math>ion concentration of an aqueous solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>001</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Since.  we know that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>log</mi><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced></math><br />\nUsing this equation, by plugging in the values</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>log</mi><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo><mo> </mo><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math><br />\nWe know that<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pOH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi>pOH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da40288e3901faea639e8"},{"id":51267,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An excess of $\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}(\\mathrm{~s})$ is added to a $5 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ solution. The concentration of $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$in the solution is closest to\n$\\left[\\right.$ Solubility product for $\\left.\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}=1.1 \\times 10^{-12}\\right]$","options":["$2.2 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{M}$","$1.5 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{M}$","$5.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$1.1 \\times 10^{-12}=\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right]^{2}\\left[5 \\times 10^{-13}\\right]\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right]=1.5 \\times 10^{-5}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213ce798f9d2d8914b1"},{"id":51268,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Ionic Equilibrium","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The pH of 0.1 M aqueous solutions of $\\mathrm{NaCl}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$ and $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$ will follow the order","options":["$\\mathrm{NaCl} \\lt  \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa} \\lt  \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$","$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} \\lt  \\mathrm{NaCl} \\lt  \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$","$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} \\lt  \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa} \\lt  \\mathrm{Na}^{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$","$\\mathrm{NaCl}^{4} \\mid \\lt  \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} \\lt  \\mathrm{CH}_{3}^{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} \\rightarrow$ acidic Salt $(P H \\lt  7)\\mathrm{NaCl} \\rightarrow$ Neutral Salt $(\\mathrm{PH}=7)\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa} \\rightarrow$ Basic salt $(\\mathrm{PH}\\gt 7)$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2135139311fc1851345"}]}